### Engineering standards

- Prefer GPU-friendly transforms and opacity.
- Use coherent motion tokens.
- Avoid excessive blur and visual effects.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Keep interactions responsive during processing.
- Avoid expensive animations on large batch lists.
- Never animate fake media progress.
- Do not delay actions for decorative transitions.

Motion must improve clarity and perceived responsiveness.

The application should remain visually excellent with animations disabled.

## 20. PERFORMANCE & MEDIA ENGINE EVOLUTION

Preserve the reliable existing FFmpeg WASM architecture.

Investigate:

- Improved worker scheduling.
- Better memory cleanup.
- Smarter FFmpeg initialization.
- Resource-aware concurrency.
- Lazy-loaded advanced tools.
- Incremental processing where practical.
- Image processing optimizations.
- WebCodecs for compatible workflows.
- Hardware-assisted encoding when available.
- Browser capability detection.

Do not claim WebCodecs can replace FFmpeg universally.

WebCodecs requires appropriate demuxing/muxing and support differs by browser, codec, and device.

Perform feasibility analysis before introducing new engine dependencies.

Maintain reliable FFmpeg fallbacks.

## 21. LOCAL-FIRST PRIVACY & SECURITY

This is non-negotiable.

- Local files must not be uploaded to external servers.
- No mandatory accounts.
- No mandatory cloud backend.
- No API keys required for core functionality.
- No telemetry without meaningful user consent.
- Avoid external runtime dependencies where self-hosting is practical.
- Maintain appropriate CSP and isolation headers.
- Protect against untrusted files and metadata.
- Prevent object URL and memory leaks.
- Keep project data under user control.
- Maintain offline capabilities.

Remote URL downloading is outside the initial Stator 3.0 scope. Do not introduce a downloader backend without approval.

Maintain a clear distinction between locally processed media and any optional network-requiring capability.

